+Feature: The recommended model list fits the machine it is shown on
+
+  # ROCMAI-548: on a 16 GiB machine with an integrated GPU the list offered only
+  # models of 17-62 GiB and nothing a user could run. These scenarios hold the list
+  # to the machine's memory: it says what it measured, leads with what fits, and
+  # marks what does not.
+  #
+  # The machine is simulated, so the answer does not depend on the runner. With no
+  # dedicated GPU memory to read, system memory is the budget, which is why these
+  # run only on a host with no AMD GPU (@requires-no-gpu): on a GPU lane the real
+  # card's memory takes precedence over the memory planted here and the answer
+  # would differ per runner. System memory is read from /proc/meminfo on Linux
+  # only, hence @requires-os:linux. The planted memory is honoured only by a
+  # `rocm` built with the `e2e-test-hooks` feature (what `cargo xtask e2e`
+  # builds); the steps say so when the reported memory is not the planted figure.
+
+  @id:model-list-small-machine-is-offered-a-small-model @requires-os:linux @requires-no-gpu
+  Scenario: model-list-01 - A 16 GiB machine is offered a small model
+    Given a machine with 16 GiB of memory
+    When the user lists the recommended models
+    Then the list reports 16 GiB of system memory
+    And the small model is listed as fitting this machine
+    And the models too large for this machine are not listed as fitting
+    And the models too large for this machine are marked as needing more memory
+
+  @id:model-list-machine-too-small-for-any-recommendation @requires-os:linux @requires-no-gpu
+  Scenario: model-list-02 - A machine too small for any recommendation is told so
+    Given a machine with 2 GiB of memory
+    When the user lists the recommended models
+    Then the list reports 2 GiB of system memory
+    And the user is told no recommended model fits this machine
+    And no model is listed as fitting this machine
+    And every recommended model is marked as needing more memory
+
+  # The list points the user at `rocm diagnose --model` for a definite answer, so
+  # the model it leads with has to be one that command knows by that name.
+  @id:model-list-fitting-model-is-accepted-by-diagnose @requires-os:linux @requires-no-gpu
+  Scenario: model-list-03 - The model the list recommends is one the CLI can check
+    Given a machine with 16 GiB of memory
+    When the user lists the recommended models
+    And the user asks whether the first model offered as fitting would run
+    Then the CLI recognises that model as one of its recommendations
